        "heading": "Instructions for each method",
        "id": "instructions-for-each-method",
        "text": "The Instructions card on Deploy agents generates the commands for each method below. They are built in your browser : paste a key (or create one — it is filled in automatically) and the commands show it; otherwise they show . The key is not sent anywhere by this, and this page you are reading never contains one. Every method runs the same installer as Add device . On Windows that installs the signed agent when this server publishes one, and the standard agent otherwise. The Windows Installer package ( , 0.65) is a fourth way, below; it takes the key as . Group Policy (Active Directory) A computer startup script , which runs as SYSTEM before anyone signs in: Group Policy Management → a new GPO → Computer Configuration → Policies → Windows Settings → Scripts (Startup/Shutdown) → Startup → PowerShell Scripts → add the file, and link the GPO to the OU. Computers install on their next restart. The script lives in SYSVOL, which every computer and user in the domain can read. Give the key a use limit near the number of computers and an expiry near the end of the rollout, and revoke it afterwards. Intune Platform script (simplest): Devices → Scripts and remediations → Platform scripts → Add → Windows 10 and later , the same file, run as the logged on user: No , 64 bit PowerShell: Yes . Win32 app (for a detection rule and retries): package the file with , install command , install behaviour System , and a custom detection script: . Line of business app (the .msi, below): upload and put in Command line arguments . Windows Installer package (.msi, 0.65) It has never been run on real Windows. It was built and read back on Linux ( , ) and its parts are tested there; try it on one computer before a rollout, and tell us what happened. What it is: A per machine package that carries the signed agent , byte for byte the published , in its Binary table, and one custom action. On a first install it runs that agent, deferred, as SYSTEM: . That is the same command the download runs when it is double clicked, so nothing about enrolment changes: the agent verifies the installer, RustDesk and the tray against signed metadata, and the claim decides where the computer lands. A failed install fails the (exit 1603), so Intune and the RMM see it. It needs a signed agent. Only the signed agent takes a token on its command line. A server that does not publish one answers ; use the startup script or the . is required on install , and must be hexadecimal: a missing one, or one with a space or a quote, stops the install at its launch condition with a message saying so. is hidden from the installer's log. A deployment key (96 hex) and an Add device code (64 hex) both work. Uninstalling it removes only its own entry in Programs and Features (and the marker it writes). It installs no files and never removes the agent. The agent updates itself from signed releases, which a Windows Installer repair would roll back, and a computer leaves through the console (see offboarding.md), not through Programs and Features. A newer package replaces an older one's entry. Downloading it consumes nothing. The computer's first check in uses the key, exactly as with every other method; a computer already enrolled uses nothing. Group Policy Software Installation cannot pass on a command line. Use the startup script above, or download a package with the key already in it ( ) and assign that. That copy carries the key in the share, like the script does, and Windows keeps a copy of every installed package in its installer cache on each computer: give the key a use limit and an expiry, and revoke it when the rollout is done. The server builds each package when it is downloaded, with and from the package ( ). A server without them answers , and Add device says so. Each build happens in its own private temporary directory, removed before the download is sent; nothing is cached. An existing RMM Anything that can run a command as SYSTEM or root (Automate, NinjaOne, Datto, Tactical RMM, PDQ, "
