        "heading": "Remote access: what the portal cannot enforce today",
        "id": "remote-access-what-the-portal-cannot-enforce-today",
        "text": "From security model.md, offboarding.md and connecting.md. None of it is a regression; all of it is the RustDesk architecture as shipped. 1. The permanent password works outside Rem0te. The installer sets . Anyone who has seen it can connect with any RustDesk client pointed at the server, and no session record is made. 2. Revoking access does not revoke a seen password. It stops new launches through Rem0te at once. Rotation is staged and applied at the next check in; until then the old password works, and an offline computer stays exposed. Linux and macOS cannot be rotated from the console. 3. View only and consent are not enforced by the endpoint. For preset technicians the portal refuses them ( , ), so nothing weaker is substituted. For owners and admins they are \"not applied\". 4. Clipboard and file transfer are RustDesk client toggles; Rem0te enforces neither. 5. The portal cannot end a session , and session length is advisory. 6. Session evidence is thin. \"Connection confirmed\" counts processes; there is no record of what was done. These are design problems, not test gaps: Phase 2 of the roadmap."
