        "heading": "Known limits",
        "id": "known-limits",
        "text": "Windows only, and Microsoft updates only. No third party patching or feature upgrades. Drivers (0.42) install as Windows Update offers them; there is no rollback of a driver from here. Installing a driver or a definition update (0.42) has been proven against the simulated Windows Update only. On a real computer it is the same call as any other update, unattended ( ), but a driver that does stop to ask has not been seen yet. The in window restart has not yet run on a real Windows computer. Its warning is Windows' own shutdown dialog; there is no other notice and no postponing by the user. A deferred install window restart is retried only by a policy's restart window (0.54). No uninstall or rollback. Rings are chosen by hand for a deployment and for a hand made policy. A company wide or assignable policy (0.50) builds them in enrolment order. There is no grouping by model, site or role, and device groups are not a policy scope. A company wide policy's settings are fixed for its life; to change them, retire it and create another. It names at most 101 computers. A change of a computer's Patch policy reaches the signed policy only by revision, so it can take the debounce plus up to an hour (more if a deployment is running, and until two approvals in signed mode). Exclude takes effect at once. The Devices list doesn't show each computer's patch policy; the Patches tab and the business's policy view do. The session check sees RustDesk sessions only, once. A signed in user, at the console or over Remote Desktop, does not stop a restart: they see the warning, and unsaved work is lost. A scheduled install (0.52) starts at the computer's first check in after its time, so up to a few minutes late, and not at all if the computer is off for the whole window. Scheduling has not run on real Windows. (0.53 added monthly windows; 0.54 restart windows.) Under a policy, ring results are signed by the computers (0.34), and a forged result is refused. Each computer's key is trusted on first use: the first time a policy names it, approvers vouch for it, and only a fingerprint read back on the computer proves it (The trust on first use window). Under a policy, a computer whose result is missing counts as failed, even one that was canceled before delivery. A computer managed by WSUS or Intune is refused rather than coordinated with. Posture is device reported, so a compromised computer can misreport its own posture. It can't widen another computer's plan, and it can't get anything installed that Windows Update doesn't offer it. A compromised portal can delay, cancel or withhold a ring, or misreport the gate to you . It can't sign a plan, add a computer to a signed ring, or make a computer install an update the plan doesn't name."
