        "id": "what-has-been-proven-and-what-hasnt",
        "text": "Level State Fixture tested Yes. Go tests cover every refusal, replay, crash and verification path. (99 checks from 0.34; 79 in 0.32) covers synthetic passkeys through the real WebAuthn verification: forged origin and RP ID, missing user verification, reused and cross decision challenges, counter regression, the requester approving, quorum, customer sign off and restart. (76 checks) runs the portal, the signer and the agent's real decision code against a simulated Windows, including the compromised database envelopes Fixture tested, 0.40 Direct mode: Go tests ( : no approvers accepted only under the signed field, still refused under a signed pause, for a revoked script, for understated risk, with customer sign off or an unknown mode, and when the field is added after signing). (252 checks) adds the CLI, the read only policy route, admission, signing at admission, patch policy and allowlist activation, and the customer sign off exclusion. (92 checks) requests on a server in direct mode, runs it on the agent's real decision code with no approval step, and checks the fresh sign in, ClientSt0r refusal, the pause, Birch's isolation, the return to signed mode and an agent from 0.38 refusing ( ) Fixture tested, 0.32 Go tests for the maintenance actions (allowlist, charset, delay bounds, session check, journal steps, interruption, redaction and size caps); adds the maintenance admission and two approver cases; runs all four through the portal, the signer and the agent's real decision code against a simulated Windows (service manager, , and the session count are simulated) Fixture tested, 0.34 Go tests for script runs (every refusal, at most once, interruption, exit code, timeout, output cap), including runs under real PowerShell 7 on Linux ; adds the script allowlist and script run cases; runs the whole path through the portal, the signer and the agent's real decision code with PowerShell 7 Fixture tested, 0.34 Go tests for the software actions against a stub winget run through the agent's real exec path (success, already installed, hash and manifest mismatch, winget missing or too old, managed elsewhere, timeout, not effective, restart required, argument injection in every field); adds the software admission, two approver and 25 computer cases; runs the catalog, single installs and uninstalls, rings, compliance and isolation through the portal, the signer and the agent's real decision code. winget, App Installer under SYSTEM, the uninstall registry and file versions are simulated Fixture tested, 0.45 Service control and ending a process: Go tests ( : closed parameters, the refusal lists, every operation with read back, no change, failures, the RustDesk session check, a reused process id, trees, the signed pause, interruption, direct plans). (109 checks) compares the portal's parameters, charsets and protected lists with the agent's Go source. (96 checks) runs every operation in direct and signed mode through the portal, the signer and the agent's real decision code against a simulated service manager and process table, including plans a compromised portal would forge. The service control manager and OpenProcess / TerminateProcess calls have not run on real Windows Fixture tested, 0.52 Task progress and scheduled installs: and (see Staged patching), against the simulated agent. Scheduling has not run on real Windows Paired lab (real Windows workstation, real authenticator, real ClientSt0r) Not done. (0.34: nor Windows PowerShell 5.1, the SYSTEM context or the job object for script runs, nor winget under SYSTEM for the software actions.) The Windows calls (service control manager, registry product type, , and from 0.32 , and the RustDesk process count) are compiled and vetted for Windows but haven't run on a lab machine Production ready No. It needs the paired lab first, and an operator who has done the key and roster ceremonies"
