        "heading": "What this release does",
        "id": "what-this-release-does",
        "text": "Scripts Name, scope ( global , set by a Platform Admin, or one business ), and a list of versions Versions Immutable. Each holds the content, its SHA 256, description, language (PowerShell), target OS (Windows), risk level and declared parameters. Editing makes a new version; nothing changes a version once it is written, and the database refuses to change it too Review Draft → In review → Approved or Rejected. Approved and Rejected are final. The approver must be someone other than everyone who wrote the content, and must hold the review permission. Approving needs a fresh sign in. The approval records the hash the reviewer saw Static checks Shown to the reviewer as warnings: , download and run, , credential material, disabling Defender, , restarts, hidden Unicode and more. They flag ; they never block and never mean a script is safe Diff Any two versions of a script, line by line Running (0.34) An approved version's page has a Run this version panel: propose it to the approval signer's allowlist, then request runs on up to 25 Windows computers with typed arguments and a timeout. Results, with the exit code and redacted output, appear on each computer's Actions tab Scheduling (0.58) Once: the Run panel takes a date and time in a named zone, up to 14 days ahead, as the signed plan's . Recurring: Run it on a schedule makes an automation schedule — every few hours, daily, weekly or monthly — that two signer approvers (not the author, not anyone who wrote the script) approve once, and under which the signer signs each run Audit Every create, submit, withdraw, approval, rejection and refused review attempt, every allowlist proposal and revocation, and every run (requested, approved, dispatched, result), with the hashes It doesn't run a script on Linux or macOS, run one outside a signed plan, or collect more than 64 KB of output per stream. 1. How a script runs — only an approved version runs, as a signed action approved twice on the signer; what is proven, and that it has not yet run on a real Windows computer. 2. Scope — global scripts, or one business's. 3. A script — its latest version and review state. 4. New script — writes version 1 as a draft. 1. Version 2 — in review, written by someone other than the reviewer, edited from v1. Compare below it shows any two versions line by line. 2. Content SHA 256 — the hash an approval records. (Shown as a placeholder here: the capture masks every long hex string. The Review box repeats it in short form.) 3. Static checks — warnings for the reviewer; they flag, never block. 4. The content — exactly the bytes the hash is of. 5. Approve — offered only to someone who did not write the content, and it asks for a fresh sign in."
