        "heading": "4. Levels: ACR to LoA",
        "id": "4-levels-acr-to-loa",
        "text": "Keycloak's step up authentication maps values to levels of authentication (LoA). 1. Realm settings → General → ACR to LoA Mapping (or per client, Advanced → ACR to LoA Mapping ): for example → 1, → 2, → 3. Using the numbers themselves as values keeps it simple. 2. Authentication → Flows : copy browser and build the conditional levels ( Condition Level of Authentication ): level 1 username and password; level 2 adds OTP; level 3 a WebAuthn passkey ( WebAuthn Passwordless Authenticator ). Bind the copy as the browser flow. 3. Make sure the ID token carries : add Keycloak's Authentication Method Reference (AMR) mapper to the client's dedicated scope, and give each authenticator a reference value in its configuration — for the password, for OTP, for WebAuthn. Which Keycloak versions emit , and how, has not been checked here. 4. In Rem0te, on the provider's card: acr for password , acr for MFA , acr for passkey . Rem0te asks a step up with , and , and then checks that the ID token's and say so, that is after the round trip began, and that the identity is the one linked to the signed in account. Then decide what to trust (all off by default): Count the provider's MFA as Rem0te's second factor — Rem0te stops asking its own TOTP after a level 2 sign in. Trust the provider's passkeys as phishing resistant — a level 3 step up counts as a passkey for the few actions that require one. Let Platform Admins sign in through this provider — and then only at level 3."
