        "id": "alerts-and-tickets-029",
        "text": "rem0te can offer monitoring alerts to ClientSt0r so it opens a ticket, and show that ticket on the alert. Turn it on per business, or globally, in Settings → Monitoring → Notifications → Offer alerts to ClientSt0r , with a severity floor (critical by default). Only alerts at or above it are sent. Nothing else is needed on the rem0te side: the peer's existing receives the alert events, and the existing organization mapping decides which business is which organization. Route What it does (push) — , , Signed alert events after a sequence, for a gap The alert now Record the ticket opened for it Service level, not delegated. No technician token: nothing here acts for a person or reaches a computer. The only write is the ticket link, and it grants nothing. One ticket per alert. A different ticket for an already linked alert is ; changing it is done in rem0te. What ClientSt0r learns. The alert title, severity, state, the computer's and site's names, the rem0te computer id and the times. Not inventory, not event log text beyond the alert title. When it is sent. goes out with the first notification level, so it waits like a notification does: offline alerts are held for three minutes, an alert grouped under an open site outage is never sent on its own (the outage is), and a maintenance window holds it until the window ends — and then only if the alert is still open. Planned work does not open tickets. Containment does not stop alert events. They report; they change nothing on a computer. Only the kinds the contract names (0.36). is an enum in the contract; an alert of a kind outside it is not signed or sent at all. The 0.36 agent silent monitor ( ) is such a kind: with ticketing on it is notified as usual but never offered to ClientSt0r. Carrying it means adding it to the enum — a contract revision ClientSt0r must accept first, since a strict validator would refuse the event. That revision is proposed, not agreed (revision 5, §5B, 0.36.1): alerts and a signed event for tray requests, sent only to a peer that declares it implements revision 5. Nothing of it is built, and rem0te sends neither today. Patch summary (0.30). A restart pending alert carries , a redacted summary of the computer's Patches tab: evidence source and age, freshness, last boot, who manages patching, restart pending, pending KB numbers and which KBs were confirmed by a later scan, failed or await confirmation. No update titles, error text, identifiers, user names or addresses. See What each agent can do."
