    "html": "<h1 id=\"compatibility-testing\">Compatibility testing<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#compatibility-testing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h1>\n<p>Every release must keep working with the agents already installed. Nothing\nforces a computer to update on the day a release ships: the signed agent\nchecks once a day, a computer may be off for weeks, and an update can be\npaused. So a server release is tested against the <strong>older released agents</strong>\nstill in use, not only against the agent built from the same commit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-runs\">What runs<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#what-runs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<div class=\"docs-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>What it proves</th>\n<th>Evidence level</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>compat (agent vX.Y.Z)</code> CI jobs</td>\n<td>the signed agent&#39;s decision code <strong>as released at that tag</strong> still works with this server: approved actions, staged patching and patch policies, maintenance actions, service and process control, technician presets</td>\n<td><strong>Sim</strong> — the agent&#39;s real Go code against <code>rem0te-action-sim</code>&#39;s fake Windows services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The ordinary e2e suites</td>\n<td>the agent built from this commit works with this server</td>\n<td>Sim / API</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>agent-lab-windows</code> (when merged)</td>\n<td>the agent&#39;s Windows code and RustDesk on a real, disposable Windows Server VM</td>\n<td>Lab</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>None of the compatibility jobs touch a real Windows computer. A green\ncompatibility run says the older agent&#39;s <em>decisions</em> still meet the new\nserver&#39;s plans, results and refusals. It does not say the agent&#39;s Windows\ncalls work; that is what the lab and the fleet are for\n(<code>docs/readiness.md</code> and <code>docs/lab-testing.md</code>, both in review).</p>\n<h3 id=\"new-report-fields-069\">New report fields (0.69)<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#new-report-fields-069\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h3>\n<p>A signed agent from 0.69 adds one heartbeat field, <code>rustdeskPreflight</code>\n(Managed Secure Access readiness). Older agents send nothing and get exactly\nthe answer they got before — no new key in it. The portal drops a block it\ncannot parse rather than refusing the heartbeat, so an agent newer than the\nportal never fails either. <code>e2e-msa-readiness.mjs</code> checks both.</p>\n<h2 id=\"supported-versions\">Supported versions<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#supported-versions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>scripts/compat/supported-agents.json</code> lists them, each with why it is there:</p>\n<div class=\"docs-table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Agent</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>v0.64.0</td>\n<td>running on production computers (2026-11-19)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>v0.67.0</td>\n<td>the previous minor release before 0.68</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>v0.68.3</td>\n<td>the previous release (0.69 adds the RustDesk preflight; 0.68.3 sends none)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The agent built from the commit under test is always covered by the ordinary\nsuites.</p>\n<p><strong>Changing the list is a support decision.</strong> A version leaves only by moving\nto <code>retired</code> with the date and the reason, so the record of what was once\nsupported stays. Add the previous release on every release, and any version\nstill reported by computers you manage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-an-older-agent-fails\">When an older agent fails<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#when-an-older-agent-fails\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A supported agent failing a suite fails the job. Either the server broke it\n(fix the server), or the change is intentional: then the suite goes in\n<code>knownIncompatible</code> for that tag, with the reason and what the computers on\nthat version must do, and the release notes say so. Nothing is dropped\nsilently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"running-it-yourself\">Running it yourself<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#running-it-yourself\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">DATABASE_URL=postgresql://…/&lt;name containing scratch or test&gt; scripts/compat-agents.sh v0.64.0\n</code></pre>\n<p>It refuses any other database, builds the agent from the tag with\n<code>git archive</code> (the working tree is not used), and writes\n<code>.compat-logs/&lt;tag&gt;/evidence.json</code>: the tag, the server version and commit,\nand each suite&#39;s result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-not-covered-yet\">What is not covered yet<a class=\"docs-anchor\" href=\"#what-is-not-covered-yet\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The standard (PowerShell) agent and the Linux and macOS agents.</strong> Their\nheartbeat and command-result bodies per released version, replayed against\nthe new API, are next.</li>\n<li><strong>RustDesk client versions</strong> against the server&#39;s connect paths: part of the\nlab.</li>\n<li><strong>ClientSt0r contract versions:</strong> the integration does not yet interoperate\nwith the real ClientSt0r (<code>docs/readiness.md</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>The release gate.</strong> The jobs report on every pull request; making them a\nrequired check is the owner&#39;s call once they have run stably.</li>\n</ul>\n",
